00:03.800 --> 00:05.000
So what is the function?

00:05.000 --> 00:05.240
Null.

00:05.280 --> 00:11.640
If null if going to go and compare two values and it's going to return a null if they are equal.

00:11.680 --> 00:16.080
Otherwise if they are not equal it again returns the first value.

00:16.120 --> 00:16.360
Okay.

00:16.400 --> 00:20.120
So now the syntax of the null if it accepts only two values.

00:20.160 --> 00:21.600
Value one and value two.

00:21.640 --> 00:26.600
So here again of course you can go and use a column with a static value like the unknown.

00:26.600 --> 00:30.560
So we are comparing the values between a column and a static value.

00:30.680 --> 00:35.040
Or you can go and compare two columns the shipping address and the billing address.

00:35.080 --> 00:37.200
So again here it accepts only two values.

00:37.200 --> 00:41.800
We cannot have it like that coalesce where we have a list of multiple values.

00:41.840 --> 00:42.120
All right.

00:42.120 --> 00:44.640
So now let's understand exactly what do we mean with the null.

00:44.680 --> 00:44.960
If.

00:45.040 --> 00:50.320
So the workflow can be like this SQL can go and check two values the value one and the value two.

00:50.600 --> 00:54.480
And if they are equal then SQL can go and return a null.

00:54.520 --> 00:59.560
But if the two values are not equal it's going to go and return the first value.

00:59.560 --> 01:01.510
So it is the one on the left side.

01:01.510 --> 01:06.070
So by checking the outcomes here, we will never have a scenario where we're going to get the second

01:06.070 --> 01:06.430
value.

01:06.430 --> 01:09.710
That means the second value always used as a check.

01:09.710 --> 01:12.070
So we are checking against this value.

01:12.070 --> 01:14.430
So either we're going to get the value one or a null.

01:14.470 --> 01:15.870
Let's have this very simple example.

01:15.870 --> 01:20.950
We are saying null if price and we are checking whether it's equal to minus one.

01:20.950 --> 01:26.230
So we are saying if the price is equal to minus one, then go and replace it with a null because it

01:26.230 --> 01:30.870
is a data quality issue that we have a price that is negative, it makes no sense for our business,

01:30.870 --> 01:33.590
and if it is minus one, then it means for us a null.

01:33.590 --> 01:38.150
We don't know the price of this product, so we will correct it using the null.

01:38.190 --> 01:38.470
If.

01:38.590 --> 01:39.950
Let's check this very simple example.

01:39.950 --> 01:44.270
We have two orders, so SQL can start with the first order and check the first value.

01:44.270 --> 01:46.510
So what is the first value is the price.

01:46.510 --> 01:48.710
So here we have a 90 SQL going to go.

01:48.710 --> 01:51.230
And check is 90 equal to minus one.

01:51.230 --> 01:55.350
Well no that means it's going to go and execute this path.

01:55.350 --> 02:00.100
So that means in the output we will get the first value which is 90.

02:00.100 --> 02:02.820
So in the output we will get a 90.

02:02.860 --> 02:04.500
Now let's move to the second order.

02:04.500 --> 02:06.020
Here we have a minus one.

02:06.020 --> 02:11.060
So SQL can check is minus one here equal to the minus one that we have in the Nullif.

02:11.260 --> 02:12.060
Well yes.

02:12.060 --> 02:17.420
So that means it's going to go and execute this path where we're going to get the null value in the

02:17.420 --> 02:17.860
output.

02:17.900 --> 02:19.500
And we're going to get it like this.

02:19.500 --> 02:24.300
So now if you compare the result from Nullif and the price, you can see we don't have any more the

02:24.300 --> 02:24.980
minus one.

02:24.980 --> 02:28.700
And as you can see now we are doing exactly the opposite as coalesce and Isnull.

02:28.700 --> 02:32.100
We are replacing a real value with a null.

02:32.140 --> 02:36.580
Now moving on to the second example, and this is a very interesting one in the analytics where we can

02:36.580 --> 02:39.500
go and use two columns inside the nullif.

02:39.500 --> 02:44.220
So in this example we are saying null if original price and discount price.

02:44.220 --> 02:47.540
So SQL have to go and compare the prices between those two columns.

02:47.540 --> 02:49.820
And if they are equal it should return a null.

02:49.860 --> 02:54.980
And now you might say okay in this example why we are doing this, well, we can use it in order to

02:55.020 --> 02:58.500
highlight or flag special cases inside our data.

02:58.500 --> 03:05.210
And the special case here is if the original price is equal to the discount price, and if those two

03:05.250 --> 03:10.570
