So let's see what's going to happen for the first row.

03:13.930 --> 03:18.810
We're going to go and compare the 150 from the original price with the discount price.

03:19.010 --> 03:20.610
So they are not equal right.

03:20.610 --> 03:26.490
So that means SQL is going to go and return the original price the 150 in the output.

03:26.490 --> 03:28.130
So let's move to the second order.

03:28.250 --> 03:30.490
Here we have the original price 250.

03:30.490 --> 03:33.370
And as well the discount price is 250.

03:33.410 --> 03:34.330
So they are equal.

03:34.330 --> 03:38.210
And if they are equal then we will get a null in the output.

03:38.210 --> 03:41.490
So as you can see again here we are not getting any values from the discount.

03:41.490 --> 03:43.410
We are using it only for a check.

03:43.410 --> 03:48.930
So with that we have a quick flag, like using the nulls as flag in order to identify where we have

03:48.970 --> 03:49.930
equal values.

03:49.930 --> 03:51.890
So this is how the null works.

03:55.850 --> 04:01.110
All right friends here we have a very nice use case for the nullif and that is preventing the error

